![](Weight%20Tracker_image.png)

The `Weight Tracker` is a [Script API](#root/_help_GLks18SNjxmC) showcase present in the [demo notes](#root/_help_wX4HbRucYSDD).

By adding `weight` as a [promoted attribute](#root/_help_OFXdgB2nNk1F) in the [template](#root/_help_KC1HB96bqqHX) from which [day notes](#root/_help_l0tKav7yLHGF) are created, you can aggregate the data and plot weight change over time.

## Implementation

The `Weight Tracker` note in the screenshot above is of the type `Render Note`. That type of note doesn't have any useful content itself. Instead it is a placeholder where a [script](#root/_help_CdNpE2pqjmI6) can render its output.

Scripts for `Render Notes` are defined in a [relation](#root/_help_zEY4DaJG4YT5) called `~renderNote`. In this example, it's the `Weight Tracker`'s child `Implementation`. The Implementation consists of two [code notes](#root/_help_6f9hih2hXXZk) that contain some HTML and JavaScript respectively, which load all the notes with a `weight`attribute and display their values in a chart.

To actually render the chart, we're using a third party library called [chart.js](https://www.chartjs.org/)which is imported as an attachment, since it's not built into Trilium.

### Code

Here's the content of the script which is placed in a [code note](#root/_help_6f9hih2hXXZk) of type `JS Frontend`:

```
async function getChartData() {
    const days = await api.runOnBackend(async () => {
        const notes = api.getNotesWithLabel('weight');
        const days = [];

        for (const note of notes) {
            const date = note.getLabelValue('dateNote');
            const weight = parseFloat(note.getLabelValue('weight'));

            if (date && weight) {
                days.push({ date, weight });
            }
        }

        days.sort((a, b) => a.date > b.date ? 1 : -1);

        return days;
    });

    const datasets = [
        {
            label: "Weight (kg)",
            backgroundColor: 'red',
            borderColor: 'red',
